WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.158
Two hours spent. Is stopping necessarily a waste?

00:00:03.158 --> 00:00:06.316
Past investment can pull a decision forward.

00:00:06.316 --> 00:00:09.474
Arkes and Blumer studied sunk cost in 1985.

00:00:09.474 --> 00:00:12.632
They examined how prior investment affects continuation.

00:00:12.632 --> 00:00:15.789
A group tendency is not an individual destiny.

00:00:15.789 --> 00:00:22.105
Irrecoverable time remains in the past.

00:00:22.105 --> 00:00:28.421
The next resources are still available to choose.

00:00:28.421 --> 00:00:34.737
What future gains and costs follow each option?

00:00:34.737 --> 00:00:41.053
Separate past and future to compare them.

00:00:41.053 --> 00:00:44.211
Sunk cost does not mean every investment is useless.

00:00:44.211 --> 00:00:47.368
Skills, refunds and commitments may affect the future.

00:00:47.368 --> 00:00:50.526
Stopping and continuing both need reasons.

00:00:50.526 --> 00:00:56.842
A paper castle can shrink or become a neighborhood.

00:00:56.842 --> 00:01:03.158
Compare tomorrow’s use, not only yesterday’s hours.

00:01:03.158 --> 00:01:06.316
Real decisions include safety and responsibility.

00:01:06.316 --> 00:01:09.474
Honor the effort without letting it decide everything.

00:01:09.474 --> 00:01:12.632
Keep the experience. Let the future guide the direction.

00:01:12.632 --> 00:01:15.789
The next step is still yours to consider.
